     <article class="story single" data-beat="positive">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">POSITIVE</span> · The Better India · 2026-10-01 · editor 6/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Bombay mill workers' 1890 protest for Sunday rest laid foundation for national social security</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#History</span> <span>#Labour</span> <span>#India</span> <span>#Social Security</span></div>
       <p>On 24 April 1890, more than 10,000 mill workers gathered at the Race Course Ground in Mahalaxmi, Bombay, to demand a weekly day of rest. Led by Narayan Meghaji Lokhande, a former mill storekeeper, the protest successfully compelled mill owners to grant a Sunday holiday by 10 June 1890. This movement marked the beginning of a long-term shift in the relationship between the Indian state, employers, and the industrial workforce.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-01/bombay-mill-workers-1890-protest-for-sunday-rest-laid-founda.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Decades later, these early labor efforts culminated in the Employees' Provident Funds Act of 1952. The scheme expanded in stages to cover major industries including textiles, cement, and engineering. Today, the Employees' Provident Fund Organisation (EPFO) manages the retirement savings of approximately 7.98 crore contributing members and oversees assets exceeding Rs 25 lakh crore. The system now provides a comprehensive safety net, including pensions and insurance, while allowing partial withdrawals for education, marriage, and housing. What to watch: Further expansion of digital services for the 82 lakh pensioners currently supported by the EPFO.</p>
       
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://thebetterindia.com/governance/epfo-history-bombay-mill-workers-protest-1890-12602825" rel="noopener">The Better India</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
