     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-10-01 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>HILDA Survey Finds Most Australians Happy in Relationships</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>The 2026 HILDA Survey, utilizing 2024 data from 17,000 Australians, reveals high overall relationship satisfaction, with couples rating quality at an average of 4.3 out of 5. Approximately 70% of participants report loving their partners &quot;very, very much,&quot; though perfect satisfaction regarding practical needs and initial expectations is lower, reported by roughly one-third to 40% of respondents.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-01/hilda-survey-finds-most-australians-happy-in-relationships.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Relationship quality typically declines after the honeymoon phase, hitting a low point approximately 24 years into a partnership due to child-rearing and financial pressures. However, data indicates a steady recovery in satisfaction for those who remain together, with quality levels rising significantly for couples after the age of 65.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Comprehensive summary of the HILDA survey findings with strong factual fidelity.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-10-australians-happy-stay.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
