       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · The Hindu · 2026-10-01 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Keralam to Enact Legislation for Elderly Welfare</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#national</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Kerala Chief Minister V.D. Satheesan announced plans to introduce comprehensive legislation providing legal guarantees to the state's elderly population. The bill, drafted by the newly formed Department for Senior Citizens, aims to promote active ageing, address loneliness, and leverage the skills of retired citizens for economic and social development.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-01/keralam-to-enact-legislation-for-elderly-welfare.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Proposed government interventions include a status survey of elderly individuals, a vulnerability registry, and the establishment of senior citizen cells in district collectorates. The state also plans to implement health infrastructure standards, a 24x7 helpline, and a Dementia-friendly initiative to provide specialized care and support through local bodies and neighbourhood networks.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Solid coverage of Chief Minister Satheesan's announcements and the new elderly welfare initiatives.</p>
