     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · The Hindu · 2026-10-01 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Madras High Court Refuses to Reject Election Petition Against Esakki Subaya</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#national</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>The Madras High Court, led by Justice P.B. Balaji, has dismissed an application by former Ambasamudram MLA Esakki Subaya to reject an election petition challenging his electoral victory. Subaya, who recently switched from AIADMK to the Tamilaga Vettri Kazhagam, sought to dismiss the challenge brought by a voter.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-01/madras-high-court-refuses-to-reject-election-petition-agains.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>While the judge allowed the removal of certain pleadings, the petition itself proceeds. This ruling is significant as Ambasamudram is among five constituencies where by-elections were previously stalled by a High Court Division Bench due to pending litigation. The Election Commission of India continues to await further judicial guidance regarding the potential scheduling of these by-polls.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article is a well-structured summary of the High Court ruling and its implications for the by-election process.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/tamil-nadu/madras-high-court-refuses-to-reject-election-petition-filed-against-esakki-subayas-ambasamudram-win/article71531436.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
