     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-10-01 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>New Research Reveals Distinctive Deepfake Victim-Blaming for Celebrities</h1>
       
       <p>New research led by Irish researchers has identified distinctive forms of victim-blaming, harassment, and barriers to justice faced by celebrity victim-survivors of deepfake sexual abuse. The study, published in the International Journal of Human-Computer Studies, is the first to pinpoint deepfake-specific victim-blaming and harm-minimizing myths within these experiences.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-01/new-research-reveals-distinctive-deepfake-victim-blaming-for.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The research found that victims endure significant psychological, emotional, social, and reputational harm. They often face claims that deepfake images are &quot;not harmful&quot; because they are fake, or that public figures should &quot;expect&quot; such content. This study, led by researchers at University College Cork (UCC) and the University of Limerick (UL), highlights how these myths exacerbate harm and fuel online harassment. Dr. John Twomey, who led the research as part of his Ph.D. at UCC and is now an assistant professor at UL, noted that speaking out can come at a &quot;real cost,&quot; with advocates risking further deepfake content.</p><p>Celebrities face a dilemma: raising awareness can expose them to more harassment. Many remain silent fearing permanent association with sexually explicit deepfakes or driving more searches for abusive imagery. The study also details major barriers to removal and justice, including time-consuming reporting processes with limited results and difficulty identifying perpetrators. Professor Gillian Murphy of UCC, a study supervisor, emphasized the need for responses that lessen the burden on victims and challenge the notion that fabricated images are less harmful.</p><p>What to watch: Future policy discussions on deepfake regulation and victim support.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article is accurate and well-synthesized, capturing the key research findings and expert commentary from the source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-10-celebrity-accounts-reveal-distinctive-victim.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
