     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · The Hindu · 2026-10-01 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Parliamentary Panel Postpones Supreme Court Visit on Simultaneous Polls Legislation</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Simultaneous Polls</span> <span>#Parliamentary Panel</span> <span>#Supreme Court</span> <span>#Separation of Powers</span></div>
       <p>A parliamentary panel examining Bills on simultaneous polls has postponed its proposed October 6 visit to the Supreme Court for an interaction with judges. Committee chairman P.P. Chaudhary announced the decision on Thursday, October 1, 2026, citing concerns raised by Opposition leaders over the separation of powers. Mr. Chaudhary also stated that the visit was put off due to conflicting dates among committee members and his own commitments.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-01/parliamentary-panel-postpones-supreme-court-visit-on-simulta.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The proposed visit had drawn significant objections, with at least one joint committee member writing to Mr. Chaudhary to request its cancellation. The member argued that discussing the constitutional validity or merits of proposed legislation with sitting judges could raise concerns about the institutional independence of Parliament and the judiciary, creating an avoidable overlap.</p><p>Independent Rajya Sabha member Kapil Sibal publicly echoed these objections, labeling the visit &quot;unconstitutional.&quot; Mr. Sibal, a senior advocate, highlighted that he had no precedent for a parliamentary panel interacting with Supreme Court judges on legislation potentially subject to future adjudication. He noted a committee program listed a &quot;local study visit&quot; to the Supreme Court on October 6, including informal discussions with the Chief Justice of India and other judges.</p><p>The joint committee is currently examining the Constitution (One Hundred and Twenty-Ninth Amendment) Bill, 2024, and the Union Territories Laws (Amendment) Bill, 2024. These Bills, introduced in Lok Sabha in December 2024, aim to establish a framework for simultaneous elections to Lok Sabha and State Legislative Assemblies.</p><p>What to watch: Future scheduling of the panel's interaction and continued debate on judicial-legislative separation.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article accurately covers the postponement, the specific reasons cited by the chairman, and the objections raised by Opposition members and Kapil Sibal.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/simultaneous-polls-parliamentary-panel-shelves-supreme-court-visit-amid-opposition-objections-over-separation-of-powers/article71533113.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
       <div class="sharebar" data-href="/article/2026-10-01/parliamentary-panel-postpones-supreme-court-visit-on-simulta" data-title="Parliamentary Panel Postpones Supreme Court Visit on Simultaneous Polls Legislation"><b>Share:</b>
