     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-10-01 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Physicists Identify 'Singular Funnels' in Complex Systems</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Researchers led by Serhiy Yanchuk have discovered 'singular funnels,' narrow pathways that allow complex systems to transition between stable states. Published in Physical Review Letters, the study explains how these structures enable systems to reach stability from starting points previously considered impossible by simpler models.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-01/physicists-identify-singular-funnels-in-complex-systems.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>These funnels exist in systems ranging from basic models to linked oscillators. While these pathways narrow as the gap between fast and slow processes increases, they never fully disappear. This finding reveals that disturbances can trigger state switches in ways that conventional, simplified models fail to capture.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Well-synthesized report on the 'singular funnels' study, covering the key scientific findings and their implications.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-09-hidden-funnels-complex-stable-states.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
