     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-10-01 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span><span class="badge quick">quick bite</span></div>
       <h1>Researchers Develop Water-Soluble Glycopolymers Using 4-Trehalosamine</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>A research team led by Saitama University's Koji Matsuoka has utilized the microbial metabolite 4-trehalosamine to design a new platform for water-soluble glycopolymers. By leveraging the C4 amino group as a chemical handle, researchers successfully synthesized a polymerizable acrylamide monomer without needing to distinguish between multiple similar hydroxyl groups.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-01/researchers-develop-water-soluble-glycopolymers-using-4-treh.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The study, published in Carbohydrate Research, confirms that this method allows for the creation of composition-tunable polyacrylamides. This approach simplifies the modification of sugar-based materials, combining the inherent biological properties of carbohydrates with the processability of synthetic polymers.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Clear explanation of the research on 4-trehalosamine and its utility in polymer chemistry.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-10-trehalose-analog-platform-soluble-glycopolymers.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
