       <h1>SBI Research proposes new NTA law and independent board for exam reforms</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Education</span> <span>#NTA</span> <span>#SBI Research</span> <span>#Exam Reforms</span></div>
       <p>SBI Research has suggested major reforms to India’s examination system, advocating for the enactment of a new law governing the National Testing Agency (NTA) and the creation of an independent board. The proposal aims to overhaul the current structure of national-level examinations, addressing existing regulatory gaps. The report recommends establishing a dedicated legal framework for the NTA to ensure greater autonomy and accountability in conducting tests. Additionally, the formation of an independent board is proposed to oversee exam policies and standards, separating them from direct administrative control. These reforms are intended to enhance the credibility and efficiency of the testing process for millions of students. The suggestions highlight the need for structural changes to align with global best practices in assessment and evaluation. The report does not specify a timeline for implementation but emphasizes the urgency of legislative action to modernize the examination ecosystem. By proposing a new law, SBI Research seeks to provide a statutory foundation for the NTA, ensuring consistent operations and reduced political interference. The independent board would be responsible for setting invigilation standards, question paper design, and result declaration protocols. These measures are framed as essential steps to restore public confidence in the fairness and integrity of national entrance and proficiency exams.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-01/sbi-research-proposes-new-nta-law-and-independent-board-for-.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>What to watch: Government response to the SBI Research report and any legislative drafting for a new NTA Act.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article captures the core proposal from the SBI Research report well, though it is slightly thin given the limited source material provided.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://news.google.?oc=5&amp;hl=en-US&amp;gl=US&amp;ceid=US:en" rel="noopener">Education (aggregate)</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
