     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-10-01 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>University of Surrey Study Criticizes 'Just Say No' Approach to Teen Sexting</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>New research from the University of Surrey indicates that blunt 'just say no' warnings regarding teen sexting fail to protect adolescents and may discourage victims from seeking help. The study suggests these rigid approaches shift blame onto victims rather than addressing the complex realities of digital intimacy and sexual abuse.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-01/university-of-surrey-study-criticizes-just-say-no-approach-t.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Researchers advocate for a 'safe uncertainty' framework that encourages professionals to respond to individual circumstances, such as coercion and emotional harm, rather than relying on punitive measures. This approach prioritizes restoring safety and dignity for young people, moving away from fragmented, ineffective responses that ignore the evolving threats of deepfakes and sextortion.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article accurately summarizes the University of Surrey research, effectively capturing the core argument and the proposed 'safe uncertainty' framework.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-10-teen-sexting.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
